Z||000000||Xtreme Fiero's||12-03-2000||09:46 PM||||<BR>I just orderd new 16X7" light weight wheels for my Nissan D21 pickup truck ad was wondering what the best tire would be to wrap them with my budget is about $100-120 per tire I have got down to 4 tires to choose from.<P>YOKOHAMA AVS Intermeiate 245/45ZR-16 $100 each (My first choice)<P>BFGoodrich Comp T/A ZR 245/45ZR-16 $110 each<P>Bridgestone Potenza RE71 245/45ZR-16 $88 each, Potenza RE730 245/45ZR-16 $114 each <P>I figured that since all of them are spots car tires that somebody here on the forum might have a set on thier car nd may have some feedback, I do think the Yoko's will stick the best but will also wear out faster. ||||reg||5
Z||000001||Shiner||12-03-2000||09:51 PM||||The RE71's wear out QUICK.  Even the other tires will be punished by a truck.  You probably don't want to hear this, but stick with something made for a truck.  It would be better in the long haul.||||reg||1
Z||000002||Xtreme Fiero's||12-03-2000||10:03 PM||||Thanks for the INFO in the RS71's. I know the Comp T/A ZR's can take the weight of my truck that weights about 3000lbs because the Comp TA ZR's come/came stock on alot of Mustangs & Camaro/Firebirds witch also weigh about that hell mu buddys Fox body mustang has 225/50/15 pep boys tires on it and that thing weighs almost 4000lbs. If I get 10-20k miles out of them i'd be happy if I wanted the to last i'd get some Pilot MXX3'3 a 245/50-ZR16 is $225 that $1,000 for a set  <IMG SRC="http://www.fiero.nl/forum/eek.gif"> I also think that a wide tier would spread the weight out nice  <IMG SRC="http://www.fiero.nl/forum/smile.gif">||||reg||1
Z||000003||LarryB||12-03-2000||10:24 PM||||I like BFG's and have had good luck with them.  You don't say if your truck is 4WD but even if it were I'd guess they would be fine as long as you don't plan any offroading. ||||reg||1
